														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4302962">
									

<p>Allow me to explain my reasoning for no-voting this article.</p>
<p>I, personally, did not have as much of an issue with the tone and disclaimer in comparison to <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/veiedhimaedhr" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3737554); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3737554&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1562788351" alt="Veiedhimaedhr" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3737554)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/veiedhimaedhr" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3737554); return false;">Veiedhimaedhr</a></span> (above). I saw it as charming, in a sense. However, despite this, there are a number of grammatical mistakes, and sentence structure could be better.</p>
<p>My main issues here, though, are the with plot and the ending.</p>
<p>The beginning starts with an interesting premise, a custom CSS theme, and a creepy image. Good start. However, as the article continues, the plot begins to boil down from &quot;strange, mysterious, world-ending anomaly that jumps dimensions&quot; to &quot;evil milk people steal milk and flood realities.&quot; That wouldn't be so bad in of itself, but the way it was executed made the article sub-par. At the end, we expect what happens, since the only logical conclusion is that &quot;oh no now we're going to die too.&quot; That is, in my opinion, the pitfall of the article, and a cliche that is too commonly used.</p>
<p>However, as much as I criticized the article, I did nonetheless enjoy it to an extent. That is why I am not downvoting it. It seems as though this piece will survive on the wiki, but I would personally recommend revamping the plot in order to tell a better story overall.</p>

							</div>

								
					
